The scroll of genocide is a major balance problem as well.  The fact
that it exists is awful from a design perspective; yet it is something 
so inherently Nethackish that I am loath to consider modifying or removing it.

> I don't mind playing a game with teleporting undead summoning
> spellcasters who'd destroy your armor and possibly instakill you if
> you don't have MR. That's one of the few points of the vanilla game I
> found interesting through Gehenom.

I can't understand how.  Everyone has MR post-castle, even if they
didn't find a source somewhere prior to the castle.  What's interesting
about Gehennom and "not having MR" at that point?

(Unsurprisingly, the answer is "nothing" -- as evinced by the parade of
complaints about Gehennom's 'boringness' throughout the years.)

> I'm exceptionally wary of playing with teleporting undead summoning
> spellcasters who'll weaken/destroy your armor even if you have MR.

Yes.  They're big, evil monsters.  You're supposed to be wary of them,
not brush them off like a piece of dandruff.  (cf. vanilla)

> poster mentioned. I think it does force you to make a choice between
> (a) the tedium of pulling together 2-3 fully ready armor kits 

Have you ever had a piece of armor destroyed by this spell?

You are significantly exaggerating the situation.  I'm not aware of 
any player who, with MR, got hit so often that a piece of armor was 
actually _destroyed_.  Why?  Because it takes four casts of the spell 
to do so, and spellcasting monsters cannot cast it every turn... much 
less four times in a row.

A careless player may well indeed get a single piece of armor destroyed.
But that player will have to have been _very_ careless.

> (b) modifying the game via ?oG to minimize the encounters 

People did that anyway in vanilla _solely_ because of the cursing and
the summoning.  But, then again, L are not the only monsters who can
cast 'destroy armor', so genocide is no longer a particularly effective
solution if that's what you're particularly worried about.

> (c) enjoying the brisk feeling of terror, exhileration, and focus 

It's hard to enjoy a game you don't have to pay attention to.

> I suppose there's always (e) becoming strong enough to instakill,
> missile kill, or distance all teleporting spellcasters within the time
> they bring themselves in range or (d) gaming the protection via prayer
> system down to -30 naked AC without the benefits of pudding farming.
> Neither is impossible, but both are not infrequently going to fail to
> happen by the time the first Master Lich shows up.

Again, this is massively overstating the case.  Neither of these options
are remotely necessary to ascend, as evinced by the 38 ascensions
currently recorded on the beta server...  not to mention that master liches 
don't teleport in Spork, and thus ranged attacks are quite viable
against them if you're _that_ worried.  

In fact, the only covetous (teleporting-to-you) non-unique monster in the game 
at this point is the arch-lich... and he's _supposed_ to be nasty.

I just don't see a problem here, sorry.

